# Check the tree out with LF endings on every platform.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the machine this is developed on, so git was handing
# the working tree CRLF while build-site.js and build-pdf.js write their
# generated blocks with LF. The result: immediately after any checkout, rebase
# or cherry-pick, `git status` listed hundreds of files as modified and
# `build-site.js --check` offered to rewrite them — while `git diff` showed
# nothing and the index, worktree and normalised blob hashes all matched. There
# was never anything wrong, which is exactly what made it worth pinning down: it
# reads like a broken generator.
#
# The repository has always stored LF. This only stops the checkout converting.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Must stay last: the rule above matches these too, and the later rule wins.
#
# Vendored pdf.js is stored byte-for-byte as published on npm, so it can be
# checksummed against the upstream tarball. Without this, core.autocrlf=true
# rewrites the line endings in pdf.min.mjs and pdf.worker.min.mjs on checkout
# and the files no longer match what was audited.
engine/vendor/pdfjs/** -text
